From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
To: Neal Frager <neal.frager@amd.com>
Cc: Gopi Kumar Bulusu <gopi@sankhya.com>
Subject: Re: glibc microblaze ____longjmp_chk.S / native gdb infinite loop

Hi Neal, Gopi,

I had my AI agent (Claude) take a look at this today. I'm on mobile and
have not been able to verify the delta myself yet, so please treat the
patch below as "reproduced and tested by the agent, not yet eyeballed by
me". Gopi's diagnosis holds up: this is a glibc bug, not a gdb bug, and
it is not something a missing glibc patch would explain. Upstream master
and the Xilinx glibc fork carry the identical file.

What the file does

  sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/____longjmp_chk.S has been this
  since the 2012 port:

      ENTRY (____longjmp_chk)
              rtsd    r15,0
              nop

  ____longjmp_chk is the machine half of __longjmp_chk, which is what
  longjmp/siglongjmp become under _FORTIFY_SOURCE. It never restores the
  jump buffer. Worse, "rtsd r15,0" returns to the address of the calling
  brald itself rather than past its delay slot ("rtsd r15,8" is the
  normal return), so __longjmp_chk calls the stub, the stub returns to
  the call, and the two instructions ping-pong forever. 100% CPU, no
  message. That is the infinite loop you are seeing.

Why gdb hits it

  gdb defines _FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 for itself (gdbsupport/common-defs.h)
  and uses longjmp to get out of readline's callback when a command
  throws (gdb/event-top.c gdb_rl_callback_handler -> throw_exception_sjlj
  -> longjmp in gdbsupport/common-exceptions.cc). So the first error a
  native gdb handles is the last thing it does. Any fortified program
  that longjmps (bash, vim, ...) hits the same wall.

Reproduction

  Bootlin microblazeel--glibc--stable-2025.08-1 (glibc 2.41) under
  qemu-microblazeel. objdump of the sysroot's libc.so.6 shows the stub,
  and a qemu -d in_asm trace shows the last translated blocks alternating
  between the brald in __longjmp_chk and the rtsd. A 10-line
  setjmp/longjmp program built with -O2 -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 hangs;
  with -U_FORTIFY_SOURCE it works.

  glibc's own tests already cover this. On a cross build of glibc master
  (10ed541ad1) run under qemu-user, debug/tst-longjmp_chk,
  tst-longjmp_chk2 and tst-longjmp_chk3 all time out. Nobody has run the
  suite on MicroBlaze; build-many-glibcs.py only compiles it.

Fix

  Delete the stub and add sysdeps/microblaze/jmpbuf-offsets.h defining
  JB_FRAME_ADDRESS, so the generic
  sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/____longjmp_chk.c gets picked up, the same way
  riscv and loongarch do. That version does the frame comparison, the
  sigaltstack fallback and the __fortify_fail call. With it, the same
  three tests pass under qemu (tst-longjmp_chk2 exercises the alternate
  stack path, tst-longjmp_chk3 checks that a bad jump aborts). A full
  make check on the patched build and a second build with
  --enable-fortify-source=2 are running now; I'll follow up with those
  results.

The patch (against glibc master, formatted for libc-alpha) is below and
attached. If AMD would rather submit it, please go ahead; otherwise I
can send it to libc-alpha once I've had a chance to look at it on a real
screen.

Thanks,
Sam

---8<---
From 7c327d4505 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 1 Sep 2026
Subject: [PATCH] microblaze: Implement ____longjmp_chk using the generic Linux version

The MicroBlaze ____longjmp_chk has been a placeholder since the port
was added in 2012:

	ENTRY (____longjmp_chk)
		rtsd	r15,0
		nop

It never jumps.  Worse, "rtsd r15,0" returns to the address of the
call instruction itself rather than past its delay slot, so
__longjmp_chk calls ____longjmp_chk, which returns to the call, which
calls it again, forever.  Every longjmp or siglongjmp compiled with
_FORTIFY_SOURCE hangs on MicroBlaze.  GDB defines _FORTIFY_SOURCE=2
by default (gdbsupport/common-defs.h) and uses longjmp to propagate
errors out of the readline callback, so a native gdb spins forever
on the first error it handles.

Remove the stub and provide JB_FRAME_ADDRESS so that the generic
sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/____longjmp_chk.c is used instead, the same
way riscv and loongarch do.

Tested on a microblazeel-linux-gnu cross build run under qemu-user:
debug/tst-longjmp_chk, tst-longjmp_chk2 and tst-longjmp_chk3 time
out before this change and pass after it.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
